To implement a transaction where multiple records are created with dependencies and an "all or nothing" requirement, the Salesforce Composite REST API is the correct tool. 

By setting `allOrNone` to `true`, the API ensures that if any of the subrequests fail (such as the `Service_Request__c` trigger failing because the Asset wasn't created), the entire transaction is rolled back. We use referenceIds to link the `Asset` to the newly created `Account` using the `@{newAccount.id}` syntax.

```http
POST /services/data/v60.0/composite HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "allOrNone": true,
  "compositeRequest": [
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Account",
      "referenceId": "newAccount",
      "body": {
        "Name": "Hollis Outdoor Supply"
      }
    },
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Asset",
      "referenceId": "newAsset",
      "body": {
        "Name": "Trailhead Kiosk 3000",
        "SerialNumber": "SN-88213",
        "AccountId": "@{newAccount.id}"
      }
    },
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Service_Request__c",
      "referenceId": "newRequest",
      "body": {
        "Subject__c": "Installation",
        "Serial_Number__c": "SN-88213"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

To perform an "upsert" (create or update) based on an external ID via the Salesforce REST API, you use the `PATCH` method on a specific SObject endpoint that includes the external ID field name and value. To associate a parent record using an external ID without knowing its Salesforce ID, you use the relationship name (in this case, `Parent`) and provide the external ID as a nested JSON object.

